Evidence and a revocation list. No SQL apply. No grant altered on either lane.
Production was SELECT only; the negative control ran on a scratch PostgreSQL 16
in the container, never on a lane.

Objects reserved: none — read-only unit.

AUDITOR'S FIGURES CONFIRMED, all four, production 2026-09-03 09:09:46Z:
  387 functions in public          -> 387
  305 anon-executable              -> 305
  222 reachable through PUBLIC     -> 222  (TRAP-BOTH: anon named AND PUBLIC)
  24 with proacl NULL              -> 24   (TRAP-PUBLIC-ONLY)
And the classification reconciles: 222 + 24 + 59 = 305, the 59 being the
functions where REVOKE FROM anon does work (ANON-NAMED-ONLY).

ONE CORRECTION, to the framing rather than the arithmetic: the trap population
is 246, not 222. The 24 NULL-ACL functions fail identically — proacl NULL does
not mean "no grants", it means "the defaults", and the default for a function
is EXECUTE TO PUBLIC. A gate written against 222 leaves 24 open and reports
success. 162 of the 246 are VOLATILE, so two thirds of the trap sits on the
amplification class rather than on read helpers.

PHASE 1 TARGET LIST COMPLETE, 09:10:20Z. The Auditor's two examples are
confirmed and they are the ONLY two of the 15 named targets:
  search_certificates                   -> REVOKE FROM PUBLIC as well
  recompute_entry_from_tag_assignments  -> REVOKE FROM PUBLIC as well
The other 11 revoke as written; enqueue_email and read_email_batch are already
closed (anon and authenticated both false, measured). Phase 1 is not blocked
and needs no re-planning — two extra lines and a verification step.

THE TRAP PROVED, on fixtures matching all three classes. REVOKE FROM anon on a
PUBLIC-granted function succeeds, reports success, and leaves anon able to
execute; REVOKE FROM PUBLIC closes it. On the NULL-ACL case the revoke
MATERIALISES the default into an explicit ACL that still contains =X.

SECOND HALF OF THE TRAP, found while proving the first and not asked for:
CREATE OR REPLACE preserves an ACL, but DROP + CREATE resets proacl to NULL —
PUBLIC again. A function closed in Phase 1 silently reopens the first time
somebody recreates it, and 42P13 means any return-type change MUST be
DROP + CREATE. So proving anon EXECUTE absent once is not enough; it must be
re-proved after any migration that recreates a function. The census probe is
re-runnable for that reason. A standing CI check is proposed, not built — a
workflow change is its own unit.

