+# F-62 — `REVOKE ... FROM anon` is a no-op on 246 of 305 anon-executable functions
+
+**Unit** F-62 · D1 (Database & Runtime), with the security reviewer's triage applied
+**Lane** production `jtdtehuqtinjxropkkcn` · **SELECT only** · no writes, no DDL, no grant changed
+**Instrument** `supabase/migrations/PROBE_public_grant_census.sql`, run through the Supabase MCP `execute_sql` transport, 2026-09-03 **09:09:46Z** (totals) and **09:10:20Z** (Phase 1 targets)
+**Raw output** `docs/evidence/d1/F62/public-grant-census-2026-09-03.tsv` — 305 rows, one per anon-executable function, classified
+
+---
+
+## 1 · The finding in one paragraph
+
+Phase 1 is about to revoke `EXECUTE` from `anon` on a list of functions. **On 246 of this database's 305 anon-executable functions, that statement will succeed, report success, and leave `anon` able to execute the function**, because `anon` reaches it through a grant to `PUBLIC` that the revoke does not touch. There is no error, no warning, and no difference in the psql output between a revoke that worked and one that did nothing. The only way to tell them apart is to ask the database afterwards.
+
+This generalises a defect first measured on one function — `get_top_contributors_v2`, 2026-09-03 08:53Z, during the TC-v3 unit. The Auditor re-measured it across the schema and raised F-62. This report confirms the Auditor's figures, corrects the framing of one of them, completes the Phase 1 target list, and proves the trap on a fixture.
+
+---
+
+## 2 · Confirming the Auditor's figures
+
+The Auditor measured, production 2026-09-03: **387 functions, 305 anon-executable, 222 reachable by anon through PUBLIC, plus 24 with `proacl` NULL.**
+
+| Auditor | D1 measured, 09:09:46Z | verdict |
+|---|---|---|
+| 387 functions in `public` | **387** | **CONFIRMED** |
+| 305 anon-executable | **305** | **CONFIRMED** |
+| 222 with PUBLIC named in `proacl` | **222** (`TRAP-BOTH`) | **CONFIRMED** |
+| 24 with `proacl` NULL | **24** (`TRAP-PUBLIC-ONLY`) | **CONFIRMED** |
+
+All four figures reproduce exactly. The classification also reconciles, which is the check that matters: **222 + 24 + 59 = 305**, where the 59 (`ANON-NAMED-ONLY`) are the functions on which a plain `REVOKE ... FROM anon` does work.
+
+### 2.1 One correction, and it is to the framing, not the arithmetic
+
+**The trap population is 246, not 222.** The Auditor's two numbers are both right, but they are written as "222, plus 24" — as though the 24 were a separate observation. They are the same defect and they fail in the same way:
+
+| class | n | what `REVOKE ... FROM anon` does | why |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `TRAP-BOTH` | **222** | removes the named grant, **anon still executes** | PUBLIC also holds EXECUTE |
+| `TRAP-PUBLIC-ONLY` | **24** | removes a grant that was never there, **anon still executes** | `proacl` is NULL, and NULL means *the default*, and the default for a function is EXECUTE to PUBLIC |
+| `ANON-NAMED-ONLY` | **59** | closes it | PUBLIC does not hold EXECUTE |
+
+**Any gate written against 222 leaves 24 functions open and reports success.** The gate must be written against 246 — or, better, against none of these numbers at all: against `has_function_privilege('anon', oid, 'EXECUTE') = false`, measured after the change, per function. That is what the Auditor's gate sentence already says, and this correction is only about which number appears beside it.
+
+`proacl IS NULL` is the more dangerous of the two, and it is worth saying why plainly: **a NULL ACL reads as "no grants" and means "the most permissive defaults".** A reviewer scanning `proacl::text` for role names sees an empty cell and moves on.
+
+### 2.2 What else the census shows
+
+Of the 246 in the trap, **162 are `VOLATILE`** (69 `STABLE`, 15 `IMMUTABLE`). Volatile plus anon-executable is the amplification class — the one that costs an attacker nothing and the database a lot. So the trap is not evenly spread across harmless read helpers; two thirds of it sits on the functions that do work.
+
+---
+
+## 3 · Which Phase 1 targets actually need `REVOKE FROM PUBLIC`
+
+Measured per function, 09:10:20Z. **The Auditor's two examples are confirmed, and the list is complete: they are the only two.**
+
+| unit | function | volatility | PUBLIC holds EXECUTE | required action |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| P31 | `search_certificates(_name text, _course_title text, _issued_date date)` | `s` | **YES** | **`REVOKE FROM PUBLIC` as well** — anon-only revoke is a no-op |
+| P32 | `recompute_entry_from_tag_assignments(p_entry_id uuid)` | `v` | **YES** | **`REVOKE FROM PUBLIC` as well** — anon-only revoke is a no-op |
+| P30 | `email_exists(_email text)` | `s` | no | `REVOKE FROM anon` works as written |
+| P31 | `verify_staff_id(_id_number text)` | `s` | no | `REVOKE FROM anon` works as written |
+| P32 | `_gen_competition_order_no()` | `v` | no | `REVOKE FROM anon` works as written |
+| P32 | `get_broadcast_feed(uuid[], integer)` | `v` | no | `REVOKE FROM anon` works as written |
+| P32 | `get_broadcast_feed(uuid[], integer, integer)` | `v` | no | `REVOKE FROM anon` works as written |
+| P32 | `get_broadcast_feed(uuid[], integer, integer, text[])` | `v` | no | `REVOKE FROM anon` works as written |
+| P32 | `increment_managed_page_view(_page_id text)` | `v` | no | `REVOKE FROM anon` works as written |
+| P32 | `recompute_entry_public_status(p_entry_id uuid)` | `v` | no | `REVOKE FROM anon` works as written |
+| P32 | `record_test_agent_run(… 15 args)` | `v` | no | `REVOKE FROM anon` works as written |
+| P32 | `set_write_path(p text)` | `v` | no | `REVOKE FROM anon` works as written |
+| P33-adj | `enqueue_email(queue_name text, payload jsonb)` | `v` | no | **already closed** — anon and authenticated both false, 09:10:20Z |
+| P33-adj | `read_email_batch(queue_name text, batch_size integer, vt integer)` | `v` | no | **already closed**, 09:10:20Z |
+| P33-adj | `get_primary_admin_user_id()` | `s` | no | `REVOKE FROM anon` works as written |
+
+**Report the reduction as prominently as the finding.** 246 of 305 functions are in the trap, but only **2 of the 15 named Phase 1 targets** are. Phase 1 is not blocked and does not need re-planning; it needs two extra lines of SQL and a verification step it should have had anyway. `enqueue_email` — the worst case on the standing register — is **already closed**, measured, and needs nothing.
+
+The other 244 trapped functions are not Phase 1's business. They are a standing posture problem to be handled as its own unit, and this census is the list.
+
+---
+
+## 4 · The trap, proved
+
+Run on a **scratch PostgreSQL 16 in the D1 container**, 2026-09-03. Production was not written to, not even inside a transaction that rolls back: a `REVOKE` in a rolled-back transaction still takes an `AccessExclusiveLock` on the function and still writes WAL, and the trap is a property of PostgreSQL's ACL model rather than of anyone's data. There is no reason to touch a lane to demonstrate it.
+
+Three fixtures, matching the three classes the census found:
+
+```
+── trap_both · the 222-function class ──
+  before                     anon_can_execute = true
+  REVOKE ... FROM anon;   →  anon_can_execute = true    ⚠ SUCCEEDED AND CHANGED NOTHING
+     acl now: {=X/postgres,postgres=X/postgres,authenticated=X/postgres}
+  REVOKE ... FROM public; →  anon_can_execute = false   ✓ closed
+
+── trap_public_only · the 24-function class (proacl NULL) ──
+  before                     anon_can_execute = true
+  REVOKE ... FROM anon;   →  anon_can_execute = true    ⚠ NO-OP
+     acl now: {=X/postgres,postgres=X/postgres}   ← materialised the default; PUBLIC still there
+  REVOKE ... FROM public; →  anon_can_execute = false   ✓ closed
+
+── no_trap · the 59-function class ──
+  before                     anon_can_execute = true
+  REVOKE ... FROM anon;   →  anon_can_execute = false   ✓ works as written
+```
+
+The middle case is the one to look at twice. Before the revoke, `proacl` was `NULL` and a reader would have said "no grants". The revoke **materialised** the default into an explicit ACL — and the explicit ACL it wrote still contains `=X`, PUBLIC. The statement made the permission visible without removing it.
+
+### 4.1 A second half to the trap, found while proving the first
+
+Not asked for, and it changes what the gate has to be:
+
+```
+  correctly closed         anon_can_execute = false
+  after CREATE OR REPLACE  anon_can_execute = false   (acl: {postgres=X/postgres})   ✓ survives
+  after DROP + CREATE      anon_can_execute = true    ⚠ THE GRANT IS BACK (acl: NULL)
+```
+
+`CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION` preserves the ACL, so a revoke survives a body change. **`DROP FUNCTION` followed by `CREATE FUNCTION` resets `proacl` to NULL — which is PUBLIC again.** A function that was correctly closed in Phase 1 silently reopens the first time somebody has to recreate it.
+
+That is not hypothetical here. PostgreSQL refuses to change a function's return type through `CREATE OR REPLACE` (42P13) — the constraint that forced `get_top_contributors_v3` to be a new function rather than an edit, measured in the TC-v3 unit the same day. Any signature change to a revoked function *must* be DROP + CREATE, and the revoke will not survive it.
+
+**Consequence for the gate:** proving `anon` EXECUTE absent once, at revoke time, is not enough. It has to be re-proved after any migration that drops and recreates a function. The census probe in this PR is re-runnable for exactly that reason, and a standing check belongs in CI — proposed, not built here, because CI workflow changes are their own unit.
+
+---
+
+## 5 · What this report does not claim
+
+- **It did not call any function.** Reachability is established from the catalogue — `has_function_privilege` and `aclexplode` — not by executing anything as `anon`. "anon holds EXECUTE" is a different claim from "anon can reach it over HTTP", and PostgREST's own exposure rules are not tested here.
+- **It did not change a single grant on either lane.** Every production statement was a `SELECT`.
+- **It measured production only.** Staging was not censused; its grant posture is unknown as of 2026-09-03 09:13Z and should not be assumed to match.
+- **The 59 `ANON-NAMED-ONLY` functions are not thereby safe.** They are only *revocable as written*. Whether each should be anon-executable at all is P30–P33's question, not this one's.
+
+---
+
