+F-62 · negative-control transcript
+Scratch PostgreSQL 16 in the D1 container. NOT a lane. No production statement here.
+Run 2026-09-03. Instrument: psql, fixtures created in this file's first block.
+
+server: PostgreSQL 16.13 (Ubuntu 16.13-0ubuntu0.24.04.1) on x86_64-pc-linux-gnu, compiled by gcc (Ubuntu 13.3.0-6ubuntu2~24.04.1) 13.3.0, 64-bit
+
+FIXTURES
+  trap_both         CREATE FUNCTION (default PUBLIC grant kept) then GRANT TO anon
+                    -> proacl {=X/postgres,postgres=X/postgres,anon=X/postgres,authenticated=X/postgres}
+  trap_public_only  CREATE FUNCTION, never granted to anything
+                    -> proacl NULL  (= the default, and the default is EXECUTE TO PUBLIC)
+  no_trap           CREATE FUNCTION, REVOKE ALL FROM public, then GRANT TO anon
+                    -> proacl {postgres=X/postgres,anon=X/postgres,authenticated=X/postgres}
+
+trap_both  ·  the 222-function class
+  before                      anon_can_execute = true
+  REVOKE ... FROM anon;   ->  anon_can_execute = true    THE REVOKE SUCCEEDED AND CHANGED NOTHING
+     acl now: {=X/postgres,postgres=X/postgres,authenticated=X/postgres}
+  REVOKE ... FROM public; ->  anon_can_execute = false   closed
+     acl now: {postgres=X/postgres,authenticated=X/postgres}
+
+trap_public_only  ·  the 24-function class (proacl NULL)
+  before                      anon_can_execute = true
+  REVOKE ... FROM anon;   ->  anon_can_execute = true    NO-OP
+     acl now: {=X/postgres,postgres=X/postgres}   <- materialised the default; PUBLIC still present
+  REVOKE ... FROM public; ->  anon_can_execute = false   closed
+
+no_trap  ·  the 59-function class
+  before                      anon_can_execute = true
+  REVOKE ... FROM anon;   ->  anon_can_execute = false   works as written
+
+SECOND HALF OF THE TRAP — a correctly closed function reopens on DROP + CREATE
+  correctly closed            anon_can_execute = false
+  after CREATE OR REPLACE     anon_can_execute = false   acl {postgres=X/postgres}   survives
+  after DROP + CREATE         anon_can_execute = true    acl NULL   THE GRANT IS BACK
+
+CONCLUSION
+  The REVOKE statement succeeding proves nothing. Only
+  has_function_privilege('anon', oid, 'EXECUTE') = false, read AFTER the change,
+  proves the function is closed -- and it must be re-read after any migration
+  that drops and recreates the function.
